ZTEXT001000023fd8a808ac10000000
Zero BIOS optical loader

bios.zero contains the BIOS boot policy in our own Zero language. The unified
compiler.zero --real target implements a restricted real-mode u32 subset and
hardware/BIOS ABI primitives; it does not contain a disk loader, page-table
builder, kernel layout policy or filesystem. See REAL.ztext for exact
language/ABI limits.

Compile after rebuilding the Zero compiler:
build/zero --real < bios.zero > build/bios.bin

Boot contract
The shared ISO contains a BIOS El Torito no-emulation entry at LBA 32 with
load segment zero and load count 32 in 512-byte units. Firmware loads the
16 KiB boot file at physical 0x7c00. The compiler emits an executable header
with a far jump to 0000:7e00 and the conventional 55AA boot signature.
The same ISO also contains an EFI El Torito entry; see ISO.ztext.

The loader preserves the firmware's DL boot-device value, configures optional
COM1 and attempts validated VBE true-color graphics, with VGA text mode 3
as fallback. It starts with the 32-bit protected-mode
kernel as its default. If extended CPUID reports long mode and basic CPUID
reports PAE, it chooses the 64-bit kernel instead. Missing long mode or PAE
therefore selects a working 32-bit path rather than stopping boot. CPUID is
part of the PC processor contract; this is a Pentium-class-or-newer target,
not an 8086/286/386 compatibility loader.

INT13 extended-read support is required for both paths. BIOS firmware calls
finish before entering either protected mode or long mode. Disk/read errors
print a diagnostic and stop; they never execute a partial kernel.

Native compiler bundle
Before the kernel, the loader reads the 1 MiB TOOLS.BIN extent at ISO LBA8712
in thirty-two 32 KiB requests. Each request uses a bounce buffer at20000 and
INT15/AH87 (:biosmove) copies it to400000+chunk*32768. The48-byte descriptor
table at6800 is cleared and reconstructed for every copy. A read or copy
failure stops boot. The first chunk must contain the ZRBNDL01 magic; kernel
application dispatch validates individual records before execution. The
kernel subsequently replaces the bounce area. Firmware A20 changes during
copying are harmless because the loader reenables A20 before mode entry.

Kernel reads
The ISO stores two independent kernels, each padded to exactly 480 KiB:
the 64-bit kernel at LBA40..279 and the 32-bit kernel at LBA280..519. The EFI
FAT image starts at LBA520. The selected kernel is read by fifteen INT13/AH42
requests, each for 16 optical sectors (32 KiB). Destination segments start
at2000 and increase by0800, ending at9000; every request has offset0000.
These do not cross a 64 KiB segment boundary and fill20000..97fff exactly.
The DAP is rebuilt for each of up to three attempts, because firmware can
modify its count. Failed requests issue a BIOS disk reset. No data-disk
writes occur here; the unselected kernel is not loaded or executed.

Memory ownership before the transition
0000..04ff    BIOS IVT and data area; not overwritten by our source
0800..0817    GDT: null, selected 32/64-bit code, flat data
0820..0825    GDT descriptor
0900..09ff    cleared handoff header
1000..1fff    PML4, constructed only for the 64-bit path
2000..2fff    PDPT, constructed only for the 64-bit path
3000..3fff    first page directory, only for the 64-bit path
3900..390f    DAP during reads; reused by the 64-bit page directory afterwards
4000..47ff    temporary E820 interval bank before kernel reads
4000..5fff    second/third page directories after reads; no reserve allocation
6200..63ff    VBE controller buffer
6400..64ff    VBE mode-info buffer
6600..661f    E820 descriptor, returned size and cleared scratch
6800..682f    INT15/AH87 source/destination descriptor table
6e00..6eff    compiler ABI scratch slots
6f00..6fff    real-mode Zero variables
7c00..bbff    BIOS boot file; temporary stack grows down from 7c00
c000..cfff    fourth page directory, only for the 64-bit path
20000..97fff  selected padded kernel
100000..3fffff E820-validated kernel stack pool; entry top400000 for both paths
400000..4fffff native Zero/Ezero tool bundle (1 MiB)
800000..ffffff native tool workspace, including application stack (8 MiB)

Before reading the enlarged kernel, the BIOS Data Area must report at least
608 KiB conventional RAM. A nonzero EBDA segment must start at or above98000.
An incompatible boundary produces a diagnostic before any payload read.

Before using high RAM, :memmap[continuation|buffer] invokes INT15/E820 through
the compiler's narrow firmware ABI primitive. The source enumerates the whole
map, with a256-call limit, and requires successful completion. It accepts
20..24-byte descriptors, honors the enabled bit when24 bytes are present,
ignores empty ranges and rejects enabled non-RAM overlap with1..16MiB. Relevant
64-bit range overflow is rejected. Usable intersections are clipped without
u32 overflow and merged even when the firmware's entries are unordered or
adjacent. The entire1..16MiB region must be covered. Missing E820, gaps, reserved
overlap or excessive entries stop boot; they do not silently assume free RAM.
The reserved region covers the kernel pool, bundle and guest workspace.
This is fixed-region validation, not a general physical-memory allocator.

The loader constructs all tables in Zero. Both paths disable maskable
interrupts, mask both PICs and NMI, enable A20 through port92 without asserting
reset, and load the source-defined GDT.

For 64-bit entry, the first 4 GiB are identity mapped with 2 MiB pages. This
covers a selected framebuffer above the first GiB as well as kernel memory.
Four page directories are at 3000,4000,5000,c000. All table writes remain
below the real-mode 64 KiB segment limit. The loader never calls reserve,
so its 4000..5fff arena is available for these tables. The loader
sets PAE, loads CR3, enables EFER.LME and CR0.PE/PG/WP, then immediately uses
the compiler's architectural longjump primitive. That primitive supplies
only the far transfer and x64 entry ABI, not the surrounding boot policy.

For 32-bit entry, GDT selector 8 describes a flat 4 GiB 32-bit code segment and
selector 16 a flat data segment. The loader sets CR0.PE/WP while leaving
paging disabled, then immediately uses protectedjump. It does not access
CR3, CR4 or EFER on that path. The compiler primitive performs the far
transfer, loads flat data/stack selectors and supplies the cdecl entry ABI;
it does not implement kernel selection, disk reads, tables or CPU policy.

VBE discovery accepts supported 32-bit direct-color linear modes with RGB
or BGR8-bit component masks, first preferring1920x1080, then800x600, then
compatible640..800 by480..600 modes. Firmware far pointers, pitch, video-memory size
and full framebuffer extent are validated. Failure keeps VGA text mode 3.
The detailed service, layout and fallback rules are in BIOS-GRAPHICS.ztext.

The handoff display kind is framebuffer (2) with validated VBE metadata, or
VGA text (1) with framebuffer address B8000. Boot kind is BIOS (1). The loader
clears 256 bytes; all handoff fields are u64 with upper 32 bits zeroed.
The kernel allocation field at +24 is 120 pages. Architecture at +104 is 32 or64.
Stack-pool base at +112 is100000 and size at +120 is300000 (hexadecimal).
Bundle base/size at +128/+136 are400000/100000; workspace base/size at
+144/+152 are800000/800000 (hexadecimal). See portability/guest/BUNDLE.ztext.
Two child stacks occupy the lower pool; the main kernel frame stays at its top.
The 64-bit kernel receives the handoff in RCX. The 32-bit kernel receives it
as cdecl arg0 at [ESP+4], with a halt-return address at [ESP] and zero arg1 at
[ESP+8]. Each kernel owns display, keyboard, serial and ATA I/O after entry;
neither calls BIOS from its protected/long mode.

Target and limits
This is a 32/64-bit x86 virtual-machine optical boot path with at least 128 MiB
RAM and a CPUID-capable processor. Firmware boot mode and CPU execution width
are independent: BIOS can launch either kernel, and long mode is not required
for the 32-bit selection. This is not an 8-bit CPU image.
The fixed low-memory layout assumes the conventional PC memory model, checks
the BDA/EBDA boundary and validates its high pool with E820. It does not allocate
arbitrary firmware memory. It does not install to a hard disk, implement a
legacy MBR, or turn the optical ISO into a hybrid USB image. CPU/firmware
requirements and fixed binary-size bounds are checked by the build/loader.
Physical hardware compatibility is not established by VM tests.

Historical384KiB layout probes and low-RAM refusal are recorded in
verification/boot-growth-0.7.0/results.ztext. The full384KiB slot was tested on
BIOS32 without LM/PAE and BIOS64, including its final byte and64KiB arena.

The historical 448 KiB capacity-only probes remain in
verification/boot-capacity-448k/. Current 480 KiB slots and the native compiler
bundle are checked in verification/guest-bundle-480k/; the probe verifies the
whole 1 MiB loaded bundle, every 8 MiB workspace page and the final kernel
byte on BIOS/UEFI, both 32- and 64-bit. Native compiler execution evidence is
separate in verification/guest-app/bundle/. Final OS integration results
remain separate in verification.ztext.
